# cron.example — DOCUMENTATION ONLY, never installed automatically.
#
# Every line in this file is commented out on purpose. No script in this
# repository writes /etc/cron.d entries or calls crontab; installing cron
# is always a deliberate, manual administrator action.
#
# WHEN TO USE THIS FILE: only on a host WITHOUT systemd. The single
# production scheduling authority is the systemd tick timer
# (seo-runner-tick.timer -> seo-runner-tick.service -> worker --once,
# deploy/systemd/). Cron and systemd are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE: never enable
# the tick timer and a cron entry for the same runner on the same host —
# the runner's lock keeps that safe (second instance exits 4), but it
# produces duplicate audits and alert noise.
#
# The cron line below is the exact cron equivalent of the tick timer, and
# it is the ONLY supported cron model: one tick, every 5 minutes. Audit
# times are not configured here — they live in the runner's own schedules
# (`seo-audit-runner schedule create|enable ...`), and the same tick also
# retries queued Slack notifications. Do NOT add extra cron lines for
# `run --all` or `retry-notifications`; that re-introduces the multiple
# scheduling authorities this model exists to prevent.
#
# Cron caveats vs. systemd: cron runs in the HOST timezone (set the host
# with `timedatectl set-timezone Africa/Cairo` if you care, though
# schedules carry their own IANA timezone), there is no Persistent=
# catch-up (a tick missed while the host was down is simply skipped — the
# runner's own 24h catch-up window covers it), and output goes to a file
# instead of journald, so install deploy/logrotate.example alongside it.
#
# To use it: uncomment the single line, then install this file as
# /etc/cron.d/seo-audit-runner (mode 0644, owner root:root).
# Format: m h dom mon dow user command

#*/5 * * * *  seo-runner  /usr/local/bin/seo-audit-runner worker --once >> /var/log/seo-audit-runner/cron.log 2>&1
